## Token signing

Membership `v2.8.0` requires a PKCS#1 RSA private key. Configure
`config.auth.signingKey` with either PEM text in `value` or the name of an
existing Kubernetes Secret in `existingSecret`. Helm refuses to render when
both are empty. `existingSecret` takes precedence over `value`; `key` defaults
to `signing.key` and must not be empty when using a Secret.

```yaml
config:
  auth:
    signingKey:
      value: ""
      existingSecret: membership-signing-key
      key: signing.key
```

The Secret must exist in the release namespace and contain the PEM text at
the selected key. Membership receives it through `SIGNING_KEY`. To supply a
local PEM file instead, pass `--set-file config.auth.signingKey.value=/path/to/key.pem`.
Keep private keys outside version control. The chart validates configuration
presence; Membership validates the PEM format and rejects the compromised legacy key.

Generate a new key with OpenSSL 3:

```sh
umask 077
openssl genrsa -traditional -out membership-signing-key.pem 4096
```

### Upgrade to Membership v2.8.0

Before upgrading, provision a new key and configure the values above. Apply the
`bind-access-tokens-to-signed-jwt` migration before replacing Membership.
Existing access tokens must be issued again and downstream validators must
refresh their JWKS caches. Follow the
[Membership signing and rotation guide](https://github.com/formancehq/membership-api/blob/v2.8.0/docs/token-signing.md)
for the rollout order. Rolling back the chart does not restore old tokens.

## Cookie and OIDC-state encryption

Configure `config.auth.cryptoKey` with a stable value that is independent from
the JWT signing key. Membership uses `CRYPTO_KEY` for cookies and OIDC state;
it does not use this value to sign JWTs. The chart does not generate or own a
Secret, and it refuses to render when the value is absent or equals the legacy
insecure value `default`.

Use an existing Secret in production. Preserve the existing signing Secret and
provision a separate crypto Secret. If both values share one Kubernetes Secret,
`crypto.key` and `signing.key` must still contain different key material:

```yaml
config:
  auth:
    cryptoKey:
      value: "" # Local development only.
      existingSecret: membership-crypto-key
      key: crypto.key
    signingKey:
      value: ""
      existingSecret: membership-signing-key
      key: signing.key
```

For local development, pass a stable file with
`--set-file config.auth.cryptoKey.value=/path/to/crypto.key`. Reuse that value
for every replica and every upgrade. Do not derive it from the signing key.

### Two-phase rollout

1. While the current Membership release is still running, provision the Secret
   and configure `config.auth.cryptoKey`. Upgrade to chart `3.9.0` first and verify
   that every Membership pod is ready with the same `CRYPTO_KEY` reference.
2. Only then upgrade Membership to the release that rejects an absent key or
   the legacy `default` value. A direct application upgrade before phase 1 can
   make Membership fail at startup.

This chart release pins Membership `v2.8.2`, which requires a secure crypto key.
For phase 1, use chart `3.9.0` with Membership `v2.8.0` and configure the stable
crypto key. Verify that all replicas use it before upgrading to this chart for
phase 2. Preserve the same signing and crypto keys during the upgrade.

Keep the old Secret until the application upgrade and its rollback window are
complete. Rolling the chart back does not recreate or recover key material.

### Rotation and restarts

Rotating `CRYPTO_KEY` invalidates existing login cookies and OIDC state. Users
must authenticate again, and in-flight OIDC requests can fail. This chart gives
each pod one key, so a rolling rotation temporarily overlaps old and new
replicas. Perform rotation in a maintenance window and ensure that all replicas
converge on one value.

Changing `existingSecret` or `key`, or changing an inline `value`, changes the
pod template and triggers a Kubernetes rollout. Changing only the contents of
an external Secret does not change the pod template. The chart's
`checksum/config` annotation covers the Membership ConfigMap, not external
Secrets. If a Secret reloader watches the Deployment, it can initiate the
restart; otherwise run an explicit rollout restart and wait for every replica.
The chart does not use `lookup`, `randAlphaNum`, `randBytes`, or any other
render-time key generation.

## Migration

### From v0.38 To v1.0.0

#### EE Licence

Membership now need a EE licence. You can get a licence from the Formance team. The licence is valid for 1 cluster.
Then configure it through the `global.licence.token` and `global.licence.clusterID` values. See [Licence configuration](#licence-configuration) for more information.

#### RBAC

Membership service contains a behavior-breaking change within the RBAC module.

Before, permissions were managed dynamically on the organization and stack with a *fallback* on the organization resource. (default organization accesses and default stack accesses). Which led to a lot of confusion and inconsistency regarding the user's permissions

The fallback has been removed from the RBAC module and is only used when a new user joins the organization.

#### Breaking changes

Membership chart now use `.global.platform.<service>.oauth.client` to generate a client and allow the ability to integrate with another chart. specific configuration can added through `.config.auth.additionalOAuthClients` value.

